layout: base.njk
title: Resources
permalink: /resources/
description: Bell foundries, pipe organ builders, and stained glass studios, along with answers to common questions.
items:
  - id: who
    q: Who can apply?
    a: Eligible applicants are 501(c)(3) organizations seeking support for church bells and bell towers, pipe organs, rose windows, stained glass, related sacred architecture, artisan training, and, in some cases, spiritual work with sound, color, and frequency. Grant amounts are determined individually. Our focus is Christian sacred spaces, though we also consider closely related work.
  - id: where
    q: Do you fund projects outside the United States?
    a: Not yet. For now we are concentrating on projects in the United States. International work may follow later, through other 501(c)(3) organizations.
  - id: size
    q: How large is a grant?
    a: There is no fixed amount. Every project is different, so every grant is set case by case, after the first review.
  - id: what
    q: What can a grant pay for, and what can it not?
    a: Bells and their towers, pipe organs, rose windows and stained glass, and the training of the artisans who keep them. It cannot pay salaries, operating costs, or debt; general building work unrelated to bells, organs, or glass; or political activity. The full lists are on the <a href="/guidelines/">guidelines page</a>.
  - id: nobells
    q: Our church has no bells. Can we still apply?
    a: Yes. An electronic carillon brings the true sound of cast bronze bells through speakers in a steeple or on an exterior wall — nothing to cast, nothing to build. It rings the Call to Worship and the Angelus, peals for weddings and tolls for funerals, and its library holds thousands of hymns, Ave Maria and Gregorian chant among them. Please mention this in your inquiry.
  - id: how
    q: How do I apply?
    a: Send a short inquiry through the Apply page, with your proposal and photographs if you have them. We will review it and reply. If the request passes the first review, we will ask for more, such as a budget, drawings, and a cost estimate. Everything is done online. There is nothing to print or mail.
  - id: decides
    q: Who decides, and how long does it take?
    a: The board reviews requests as they are received. If a request proceeds past the first review, we will ask for additional materials. Timing varies.
  - id: deadline
    q: Is there a deadline?
    a: No. Inquiries are welcome at any time of year, and we read them as they arrive.
---
